<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François-Michel le Tellier, Marquis de Louvois</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1641">1641</a>–<a href="/years/1691">1691</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Louvois, Marquis de Louvois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François-Michel le Tellier, Marquis de Louvois, was French secretary of state for war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, in succession to his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-le-tellier">Michel Le Tellier</a>. He reorganised the army, improving supply, discipline, recruitment and the system of magazines, and built it into the largest standing army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He was a leading influence on the king's wars and on the harsh treatment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> and of the Palatinate in 1689.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-michel-le-tellier-marquis-de-louvois.jpg" alt="François-Michel le Tellier, Marquis de Louvois" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Pierre Mignard I · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louvois1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louvois was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 18 January 1641, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-le-tellier">Michel Le Tellier</a> and Élisabeth Turpin. His father instructed him in the management of state affairs, and the young man won the king's confidence; in 1666 he succeeded his father as war minister. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-devolution">War of Devolution</a> (1667 to 1668) Turenne noticed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and taught him the art of supplying armies. After the peace of Aix-la-Chapelle he devoted himself to organising the French army. The years from 1668 to 1672 were, in the words of Camille Rousset, years of preparation, when Lionne laboured to find allies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> money and Louvois soldiers for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With his father, Louvois oversaw an increase in the French Army to 340,000 soldiers, which fought four wars between 1667 and 1713. According to Cathal Nolan he created the Régiment du Roi in 1663 and founded the Royal-Artillerie regiment in 1673. He reorganised the army and exercised strict control over officers, which slowed tactical and operational responses. His logistics were more successful: he improved the magazine system left by his father and built up enough grain and wagons to supply his armies with 200,000 rations a day for up to six months in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a> (1672 to 1678). He also founded the Hôtel des Invalides and reorganised the military orders of merit.</p><p>Louvois had a prominent part in the capture of Strasbourg in 1681 and in the revocation of the Edict of Nantes. He annulled a clause of the revocation allowing some liberty of conscience, with the phrase that the king wished the worst harshness on those who would not share his religion, and he claimed credit for inventing the dragonnades. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> died in 1683 he took over the ministry of public buildings, and in 1688 he began the collection of plans-reliefs of French strongholds, now the Musée des Plans-Reliefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louvois died suddenly of apoplexy on 16 July 1691 after leaving the king's cabinet, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> claimed he died while taking the waters at Balaruc, and the suddenness of his death caused suspicion of poison. Historians credit him with creating the civilian role of minister of war. French writers have compared him with Carnot, since both reorganised armies with existing resources and new systems, though his methods were unscrupulous in his private life and his work, including harsh measures against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François-Michel le Tellier, Marquis de Louvois” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Louis XIV the army grew to several hundred thousand men on paper, organised by the war ministers Michel Le Tellier and his son <b>Louvois</b>, while Vauban ringed the frontiers with bastioned forts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Versailles, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1673" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of François-Michel le Tellier, Marquis de Louvois"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1673">What was here in 1673? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
